<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- iPhone only, and portrait only. Both were declared and neither was ever built: R3 of
	     spec 08 asked for OnIdiom/AdaptiveTrigger and a max-width container, and the codebase
	     contains no OnIdiom, no AdaptiveTrigger and exactly one MaximumWidthRequest. So AC3 of
	     that spec ("content uses the width sensibly on tablet/landscape") is not met, and
	     declaring iPad meant App Review opening a stretched single column on a 13" screen —
	     plus 13" screenshots we would have to shoot to even submit.
	     Narrowing here costs nothing that works today and is reversible in any later release,
	     unlike the bundle id. Widen it again in the same commit that lands the adaptive layout. -->
	<key>UIDeviceFamily</key>
	<array>
		<integer>1</integer>
	</array>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>XSAppIconAssets</key>
	<string>Assets.xcassets/appicon.appiconset</string>
	<!-- Automatic copies land in the app's Documents/Backups (2026-10-23, owner: backups "в телефон").
	     These two keys put that folder in the Files app as On My iPhone → Diarion, so a copy can be
	     seen, shared and moved without the app; Documents is also part of the iPhone's own iCloud
	     backup, which is how a copy reaches a new phone with no setup at all. Nothing else is kept in
	     Documents: the database lives in Library and stays hidden. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Allows you to securely unlock your diary.</string>
	<!-- Removed for v1 together with the HealthKit entitlement and both localised copies:
	         <key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	         <string>Used to read sleep data and populate your diary automatically.</string>
	     Read-only was always the claim — HealthDataService passes an empty share set, so there was
	     deliberately no NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription to go with it — but the feature had no way
	     in: ImportSleepDataAsync is bound by no XAML, so nothing in the app could ever call it.
	     A permission prompt App Review asks about, for a button that does not exist, is a cost with
	     no matching benefit. Restore this verbatim in the same commit that defines HEALTHKIT. -->

	<!-- Export compliance, answered once here instead of by hand on every single build: without
	     this key App Store Connect stops each upload on the questionnaire.

	     The claim being made is "no non-exempt encryption", and this is the whole basis for it:
	     the app encrypts the user's own database on the user's own device with standard published
	     algorithms (AES through LiteDB, PBKDF2 for the portable backup passphrase, SHA for the PIN
	     hash), makes no network calls at all since the on-device AI and its model downloads were
	     removed (2026-10-21), and ships no cryptography of its own design.

	     Flip this to <true/> the day any of that stops being true — a Diarion-operated proxy, a
	     custom cipher, or encryption offered to the user as a feature rather than used to protect
	     their storage. That day also brings an annual self-classification report, which is exactly
	     why the line is worth re-reading rather than inheriting. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
